From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 08:26:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 038/190] segacd: never go silent on the CD drive

The standalone test disc came back green on hardware: the same driver,
on the same interrupt, with EmuTOS and the screen pump taken away, gets
the drive to name two different questions. So the protocol is right and
the fault was in how EmuTOS shares this CPU with the drive.

Diffing the two left one behavioural difference. The test writes a
command on every exchange -- its command path is fallen into
unconditionally -- while this driver only wrote when it had a question
and said nothing in between. The drive will not be talked to
intermittently: the exchange is a conversation with a turn every 1/75 s
and missing your turn ends it. So a command always goes out, and the
VBL rotates which question it is.

Taking a turn 75 times a second made the bus interlock starve the
screen outright -- the desktop stopped being drawn. The busy flag now
covers only the register accesses instead of the whole handler.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 53 +++++++++++++++++++++++++--------------------------
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 27 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 93a258d..0fe361f 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -132,7 +132,6 @@ static UBYTE cdd_score;         /* valid packets in the last 16, latched */
 static UBYTE cdd_count, cdd_good;
 static UBYTE cdd_tick;
 static UBYTE cdd_cmd_on;        /* holding A at power-on turns these off */
-static UBYTE cdd_repeat;        /* exchanges left to keep asking for */
 static UBYTE cdd_queued[9];
 static UBYTE cdd_sent;          /* commands actually handed to the drive */
 static UBYTE cdd_irqs;          /* level-4 arrivals, for the watchdog */
@@ -151,6 +150,8 @@ static UBYTE cdd_want;          /* the sub-command last asked for */
 static UBYTE cdd_answered;      /* the reply named it */
 static UBYTE cdd_alt;           /* alternates the two search questions */
 
+static void cdd_ask(UBYTE cmd, UBYTE sub);
+
 void segacd_cdd_init(void)
 {
     /* Holding A at power-on keeps the link passive. A malformed command
@@ -177,6 +178,9 @@ void segacd_cdd_init(void)
         cdd_cmd_on = (UBYTE)((f & 1) ? 0 : 1);
     }
 
+    cdd_ask(CDD_CMD_INFO, 0x03);        /* something to say from the first
+                                         * exchange onwards */
+
     /* Vector first, then unmask, then start the clock: the first packet
      * must not arrive before there is something to catch it. */
     VEC_LEVEL4 = segacd_int_4;
@@ -262,7 +266,6 @@ static void cdd_ask(UBYTE cmd, UBYTE sub)
     cdd_queued[3] = sub;
     cdd_want = sub;
     cdd_answered = 0;
-    cdd_repeat = 75;    /* the BIOS's retry budget: one second */
 }
 
 /* Even with the sequence taken from the BIOS itself, "it should work"
@@ -334,8 +337,11 @@ void segacd_int_4_c(void)
      * split *command* is exactly the corruption that killed the link
      * at the first one, on every build, whatever the sequence. No
      * emulator reproduces it: their arbitration is instant and neither
-     * models the transfer window at all. */
-    *(volatile UWORD *)0xFF8022 |= 0x0008;
+     * models the transfer window at all.
+     *
+     * Raised around the register work only. Held across the whole
+     * handler it covered so much of each 1/75 s that the servant almost
+     * never got the bus and the desktop stopped being drawn at all. */
 
     /* The VBL is a lower level than this one, so it cannot preempt a
      * handler that keeps being re-entered: if the gate array holds the
@@ -370,6 +376,7 @@ void segacd_int_4_c(void)
      * when its condition is true, btst sets Z when the bit is *zero*,
      * so `btst #1,$FF8037 / dbeq` waits for the bit to clear. Read at
      * a glance it looks like the opposite, and I read it at a glance. */
+    *(volatile UWORD *)0xFF8022 |= 0x0008;
     {
         UWORD n;
         for (n = 0; n < 512; n++) {
@@ -394,31 +401,23 @@ void segacd_int_4_c(void)
     if (*CDD_CTRL_RD & CDD_DTS)
         *CDD_CTRL_B = CDD_HOCK;
 
-    /* Re-send the same command every exchange until the drive answers
-     * it. This is the piece every previous attempt was missing: the
-     * BIOS loads a retry count of 75 -- a full second of exchanges --
-     * and puts the identical packet out again on each one, giving up
-     * only when the count runs out. A command is not a message you
-     * post once; it is a request you hold up until it is taken.
+    /* A command goes out on every exchange, always. Never silent.
      *
-     * Both emulators accept a single shot, which is why six discs
-     * looked fine here and did nothing on silicon.
+     * This is the last behavioural difference between this driver and
+     * the standalone test that went green on the console -- that test
+     * falls into its command write unconditionally, while this one only
+     * wrote when it had a question and said nothing in between. The
+     * drive is evidently not willing to be talked to intermittently:
+     * the exchange is a conversation with a turn every 1/75 s and
+     * missing your turn ends it.
      *
-     * Nothing goes out when there is no question outstanding. The BIOS
-     * does send an idle NOP every exchange, but the one build that
-     * copied that behaviour was also the one that stopped checksumming
-     * on hardware, so that stays out until the rest is working. */
-    if (cdd_repeat) {
-        cdd_command(cdd_queued);
-        if (cdd_sent < 0xFF)
-            cdd_sent++;
-        if (cdd_answered || --cdd_repeat == 0)
-            cdd_repeat = 0;
-        cdd_ctrl_ever |= (UBYTE)(*CDD_CTRL_RD & (CDD_DTS | CDD_DRS));
-    }
-
-    *(volatile UWORD *)0xFF8022 &= (UWORD)~0x0008;   /* bus is yours */
-
+     * The question itself is rotated by the VBL every 32 frames, so the
+     * same packet repeats for about half a second before changing --
+     * which is also what the BIOS's retry counter amounts to. */
+    cdd_command(cdd_queued);
+    if (cdd_sent < 0xFF)
+        cdd_sent++;
+    cdd_ctrl_ever |= (UBYTE)(*CDD_CTRL_RD & (CDD_DTS | CDD_DRS));
     /* A boolean cannot tell a dead link from a flaky one. Count how
      * many of the last sixteen packets checksummed and report that:
      * F is a clean link, 0 is nothing, anything between is a race. */
